\section{Transformers and Attention}

\textbf{Background reading:} Follow the \href{https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1HC_QN2mCq9bkCPzmkP8RaR3RokFQCWo9oPuU7rFyR8Y/edit?usp=sharing}{general reading instructions for Block 5}.

We will implement a multi-head attention layer, a component of transformer-based models such as GPT and BERT. For a visual explanation, see Jay Alammar's \href{http://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/}{The Illustrated Transformer}, listed in the Block 5 reading instructions.

Text models map tokens to embeddings, combine context, and use the resulting representations to predict classes or tokens. Our attention layer builds contextual representations and is one component of a full transformer such as BERT. RNNs illustrate how the same learned update carries context through a sequence, and the VG tasks connect these representations to text classification.

Start by loading the parameters that we are going to use for the implementation as follows.
<<>>=
library(uuml)
data("transformer_example")
@

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Start out by computing the query, key and value matrices for one attention head by implementing it in a function you call \texttt{qkv()}. The function should work like this.
<<eval=FALSE, >>=
# Pick out the matrix for the first attention head
Wq <- transformer_example$Wq[,,1]
Wk <- transformer_example$Wk[,,1]
Wv <- transformer_example$Wv[,,1]

# Pick out the first three words and their embeddings
X <- transformer_example$embeddings[1:3,]

res <- qkv(X, Wq, Wk, Wv)
res

## $Q
##            [,1]        [,2]       [,3]
## the    0.4722259  0.04995783 -0.5350845
## quick -0.3662435  0.12144160  0.3454785
## brown -0.1029677 -0.12728414  0.1817097
##
## $K
##               [,1]         [,2]       [,3]
## the    0.094360579 -0.203807092 -0.1851229
## quick -0.033313240  0.279012100  0.2530560
## brown -0.004457052  0.001013468  0.0133802
##
## $V
##               [,1]        [,2]        [,3]
## the    0.317318525 -0.35023010  0.13284078
## quick  0.009929565  0.04208206 -0.15412097
## brown -0.316413241  0.27717408  0.02725089
@
\newpage
\item (2p) Now, based on your query, key and value, compute the attention for the three chosen tokens. Return a list containing the attention weights in \texttt{attention} and the attention output in \texttt{Z}:
\[
A = \operatorname{softmax}_{\mathrm{row}}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right),
\qquad Z = AV.
\]
Here $d_k$ is the number of columns in $K$, and softmax is applied separately to each row. Each row of $A$ sums to one. In the lecture notation, $\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)$ corresponds to $Z$.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
attention(res$Q, res$K, res$V)
## $Z
##               [,1]          [,2]         [,3]
## the    0.012395453 -0.0212420459  0.009404870
## quick -0.003759269 -0.0008360029 -0.005108890
## brown  0.002412222 -0.0088974612  0.001147999
##
## $attention
##             the     quick     brown
## the   0.3601932 0.3080896 0.3317172
## quick 0.3088780 0.3582373 0.3328847
## brown 0.3300375 0.3360583 0.3339042
@
\item (1p) Interpret the attention weights. What does the second row mean?
\item (1p) How does the second row of attention weights determine the second row of $Z$?
\item (2p) Now we have everything in place for implementing it all as a multi-head attention layer. The layer will take in embeddings and then return a 3-dimensional embedding per word. First check your implementation using the three words below.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
multi_head_self_attention(X,
                          transformer_example$Wq,
                          transformer_example$Wk,
                          transformer_example$Wv,
                          transformer_example$W0)
##               [,1]          [,2]         [,3]
## the   -0.014189613 -0.0040299008 -0.006756286
## quick -0.009963516 -0.0010724342 -0.001996524
## brown -0.006394562 -0.0006626115 -0.002219108
@
Then run it on all words using \texttt{X <- transformer\_example\$embeddings}. The full input produces nine output rows; its values will differ from the three-word example.
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\section{Implementing a simple RNN}\label{sec:simple-rnn}

%\input{distinction_task.tex}

We will implement a one-layer recurrent neural network (RNN) using \texttt{rnn\_example}. Its construction and notation are adapted from \citet[Section~10.2]{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}. Everything needed is specified below; no reading of that section is required. Reuse the same parameters at every time step.

Use four hidden units and three output units. Start from $h_0=0$, represented as a $4\times1$ column matrix. The inputs are the rows of \texttt{rnn\_example\$embeddings}.


<<>>=
library(uuml)
data("rnn_example")
hidden_dim <- 4
@

\textbf{Hint:} Each row of \texttt{X} contains one token embedding. Use \texttt{drop = FALSE} to preserve a single-row matrix. Within each RNN step, represent the hidden state as a $4\times1$ column matrix, and check that dimensions match before matrix multiplication.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Implement a simple RNN linear unit that takes the parameters \texttt{W}, \texttt{U}, \texttt{b}, the previous hidden state \texttt{h\_t\_minus\_one}, and the input embedding \texttt{x\_t}, and returns
\[
a_t=W h_{t-1}+U x_t+b.
\]
Here $x_t$ is a column vector: transpose the single-row input embedding in R. With $p$ input features and $d$ hidden units, $U$ is $d\times p$, $W$ is $d\times d$, and $b$ is $d\times1$. The function should work as follows.
<<eval=FALSE, >>=
X <- rnn_example$embeddings[1,,drop=FALSE]
h_t_minus_one <- matrix(0, nrow = hidden_dim, ncol = 1)
a_t <- rnn_unit(h_t_minus_one, X,
                W = rnn_example$W,
                U = rnn_example$U,
                b = rnn_example$b)
a_t
##
##             the
## [1,]  0.5819145
## [2,] -2.2686535
## [3,] -0.6410312
## [4,]  1.4891931
##
@
\item (1p) Implement the hidden-state update $h_t=\tanh(a_t)$, where the activation function $\tanh(a)=(e^a-e^{-a})/(e^a+e^{-a})$ is applied separately to each element.
<<eval=FALSE, >>=
h_t <- activation(a_t)
h_t
##
##             the
## [1,]  0.5240555
## [2,] -0.9788223
## [3,] -0.5656013
## [4,]  0.9031762
##
@

\item (1p) Implement the output scores $o_t=Vh_t+c$ and the softmax probabilities
\[
\widehat{y}_{t,j}=\frac{\exp(o_{t,j}-m_t)}{\sum_{k=1}^{q}\exp(o_{t,k}-m_t)},
\qquad m_t=\max_k o_{t,k}.
\]
With $q$ output units, $V$ is $q\times d$ and $c$ is $q\times1$. Subtracting the largest score gives the same probabilities while avoiding unnecessarily large exponentials. These functions should work as follows.
<<eval=FALSE, >>=
yhat_t <- softmax(output_rnn(h_t, rnn_example$V, rnn_example$c))
yhat_t
##
##            the
## [1,] 0.3063613
## [2,] 0.2930885
## [3,] 0.4005502
##
@
\item (2p) Now we are ready to implement the full recurrent layer. It should take an input matrix X and the neural network parameters and return the hidden states and the softmax output as follows.
<<eval=FALSE, >>=
X <- rnn_example$embeddings[1:3,,drop=FALSE]
rnn_layer(X,
          W = rnn_example$W,
          V = rnn_example$V,
          U = rnn_example$U,
          rnn_example$b,
          rnn_example$c)
##
## $ht
##              [,1]        [,2]       [,3]       [,4]
## the    0.52405551 -0.97882227 -0.5656013  0.9031762
## quick -0.05951368  0.03988226  0.8241800 -0.6562744
## brown -0.08984008  0.92822217 -0.1563247 -0.6657626
##
## $yhat
##            [,1]      [,2]      [,3]
## the   0.3063613 0.2930885 0.4005502
## quick 0.2838013 0.3490452 0.3671536
## brown 0.2878002 0.3666877 0.3455121
@
\item (1p) Now, what is the value of the hidden state \texttt{h\_t} for the token \texttt{dog}?
\item (1p) Why would processing only \texttt{dog} give a different hidden state from processing the sentence up to \texttt{dog}?
\end{enumerate}

