\section{*Recurrent Neural Networks with Keras}

To get VG on this assignment, you can choose to do this task OR the task using BERT with PyTorch below. You are also free to do both, if you want. As long as you pass one of the VG assignments you will get a VG point. Although, please only write down the time it took to do the VG assignment for one of the tasks if you choose to do more than one.

\input{distinction_task.tex}

\input{general_keras.tex}

We will use recurrent neural networks for text classification. Use the general reading instructions for Block 5 for conceptual background. Chapter 6--6.2 in \citet{chollet2018deep} provides optional implementation help.

An RNN can produce an output at every time step or use only its final hidden state to produce one output for a complete sequence. Use the parameter notation from Section~\ref{sec:simple-rnn} when explaining your classifier below.

Start by downloading the IMDB dataset and preprocess it as follows.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
library(tensorflow)
library(keras3)
maxlen <- 500
imdb <- dataset_imdb(num_words = 10000)
input_train <- pad_sequences(imdb$train$x, maxlen = maxlen)
y_train <- imdb$train$y
input_test <- pad_sequences(imdb$test$x, maxlen = maxlen)
y_test <- imdb$test$y
@

\textbf{Runtime hint:} Use the time taken by the first few epochs to estimate the remaining runtime. The first epoch may include extra setup overhead. These epochs count toward your existing ten-epoch run.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Create a simple ordinary neural network for text classification in Keras using an embedding layer of dimension 16 and a hidden layer with 32 hidden states (and an output layer with one unit). Use a validation split of 20\% observations in the validation set and a batch size of 128. Train your network using Adam with learning rate 0.001 and use binary cross-entropy as the loss function. Print the model and return your validation accuracy after ten epochs. Reuse this data split and training setup for the RNN and LSTM baseline below.
\item (1p) Explain what the embedding layer is doing.
\item (1p) Now setup a simple Recurrent Neural Network with 32 hidden units and the same embedding dimension. Print the model and present your result.
\item (1p) Does your RNN classifier produce an output at every time step, or one output for the whole review? Identify which hidden state(s) feed the final output layer and explain your answer.

\examplebox{RNN parameter counts}{
Consider a simple RNN with 3 input features, 2 hidden units and 1 output, with biases:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\textbf{Parameters} & \textbf{Dimensions} & \textbf{Count} \\
$U$: input-to-hidden weights & $2\times3$ & 6 \\
$W$: recurrent weights & $2\times2$ & 4 \\
Hidden biases & $2$ & 2 \\
$V$: hidden-to-output weights & $1\times2$ & 2 \\
Output bias & $1$ & 1
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Keras groups several mathematical parameters into a single layer's count. The \texttt{SimpleRNN} layer reports $6+4+2=12$ parameters; the \texttt{Dense} output layer reports $2+1=3$. Any embedding-layer parameters are counted separately.
}

\item (1p) Using the notation in Section~\ref{sec:simple-rnn}, explain the roles of $U$ and $W$. What are their dimensions in your classifier, and where are their parameter counts included in the Keras output?
\item (1p) Using the same notation, explain the role of $V$ in your classifier. How many parameters does $V$ contain, and where are they included in the Keras output?
\item (1p) Implement an LSTM baseline with 32 hidden units and \texttt{dropout = 0}, using the same embedding dimension and training setup as above. Print your model and validation accuracy.
\item (1p) Starting from your LSTM baseline, design and test two modifications of your choice. Before training, explain what you expect each modification to change and why. Change one aspect at a time, keeping the data split and other settings fixed. Include the model summaries and a compact table of validation accuracy and training time for all three models. Discuss whether the results support your expectations. Improved accuracy is not required for full credit.
\item (1p) Using training and validation performance, discuss evidence of underfitting or overfitting. Can these results indicate the Bayes error? Explain.
\end{enumerate}


